# The plan for the Roquette bound (`NK.CFTLib.roquette_bound`)

This file contains **no declarations**; it is the written-out proof plan for the four lemmas into
which the requested lemma

```lean
theorem roquette_bound (F K : Type) [Field F] [NumberField F] [Field K] [NumberField K]
    [Algebra F K] [FiniteDimensional F K] [IsGalois F K] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime]
    (hG : IsPGroup p (K ≃ₐ[F] K))
    (T : Finset (IsDedekindDomain.HeightOneSpectrum (NumberField.RingOfIntegers F)))
    (hunr : CFTLib.IsUnramified F K) (hT : CFTLib.SplitsAt F K T)
    (hclass : CFTLib.ClassCond F K p T) :
    Module.finrank (ZMod p)
        (TensorProduct ℤ (ZMod p) (tateCohomology (NK.GlobalCFT.IdeleClassGroup.rep F K) (-1))) ≤
      NumberField.InfinitePlace.nrRealPlaces F + NumberField.InfinitePlace.nrComplexPlaces F - 1 +
        CFTLib.delta F p + T.card
```

is split.  It is Roquette's idèle computation, Cassels–Fröhlich chapter IX ("On class field
towers"), §3–4.  Nothing here uses reciprocity or the existence of a fundamental class.

## Setting

`K/F` is a finite Galois extension of number fields, `G = Gal(K/F)`, `K/F` is unramified at every
place (`CFTLib.IsUnramified F K`: the different ideal is `⊤` and
`IsUnramifiedAtInfinitePlaces F K`, i.e. `NumberField.InfinitePlace.IsUnramified` at every infinite
place — equivalently `NumberField.InfinitePlace.isUnramified_iff_stabilizer_eq_bot`: the
decomposition group of every infinite place is trivial), `T` is a finite set of finite primes of
`F` which split completely in `K` (`CFTLib.SplitsAt F K T` gives inertia degree `1`; with
unramifiedness this means `e = f = 1`), `p` is prime and `G` is a `p`-group, and
`CFTLib.ClassCond F K p T` says every ideal class of `K` of `p`-power order lies in the subgroup
generated by the classes of the primes of `K` above `T`.

The vocabulary (`NK.CFTLib.roquetteDefs`):

* `unitsNormSubgroup F K = N_{K/F} Kˣ ≤ Fˣ`;
* `normPrincipalPreimage F K = (N_{K/F} 𝕀_K).comap (Idele.principalIdele F) = Fˣ ∩ N_{K/F} 𝕀_K`;
* `normQuotient F K = (Fˣ ∩ N_{K/F} 𝕀_K) / N_{K/F} Kˣ` (as the additive group
  `𝔸 (normPrincipalPreimage F K) ⧸ (unitsNormSubgroup F K).subgroupOf _`).

## Step 1 (`CFTLib/tateCohomology_neg_one_idele_of_isUnramified`)

`Ĥ⁻¹(G, 𝕀_K) = 0`.

`𝕀_K` is the restricted product over the places of `F` of the fibres `∏_{w | v} K_wˣ`, and the
filtered union of the `T`-idèles `𝕀_{K,T}` of `NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.SIdeles` over finite
`G`-stable `T`.  `Ĥ⁻¹` commutes with filtered colimits and with finite direct sums, and for a
`G`-stable `T` the snake lemma applied to

`0 → (local units outside T) → 𝕀_{K,T} → ⊕_{v ∈ T} (fibre at v) → 0`

reduces everything to the local statements:

* for a finite place `w | v`, the decomposition group `D_w = Gal(K_w/F_v)` is cyclic (the local
  extension is unramified) and `Ĥ⁻¹(D_w, K_wˣ) ≅ Ĥ¹(D_w, K_wˣ) = 0` by Hilbert 90 combined with
  Tate periodicity for cyclic groups;
* the local units of an unramified extension are cohomologically trivial:
  `Ĥⁱ(D_w, 𝒪_wˣ) = 0` for all `i` (the norm on units is surjective, giving `Ĥ⁰ = 0`, and
  `Ĥ¹ = 0` by Hilbert 90);
* at an infinite place, `IsUnramifiedAtInfinitePlaces` gives `D_w = ⊥` and `Ĥ⁻¹(1, -) = 0`.

Shapiro's lemma for the fibre (`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.fiberIdelesRep_iso_coind`) turns
`Ĥ⁻¹(G, Coind_{D_w}^{G} K_wˣ)` into `Ĥ⁻¹(D_w, K_wˣ)`.  Existing lemmas worth using:
`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.hZero_hOne_unitIdelesOutsideRep_eq_one`,
`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.natCard_tateCohomology_fiberUnitsRep_eq_one`,
`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.hZero_localUnitSubRep_of_ramificationIdx_eq_one`,
`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.milneFourTwoExact_nonempty`.

## Step 2 (`CFTLib/tateCohomology_neg_one_iso_normQuotient`)

From `0 → Kˣ → 𝕀_K → C_K → 0` (Milne, *CFT*, Lemma 4.2) as a short exact sequence of
`ℤ[G]`-modules, step 1 and the long exact sequence in Tate cohomology give an isomorphism

`Ĥ⁻¹(G, C_K) ≅ ker( Ĥ⁰(G, Kˣ) → Ĥ⁰(G, 𝕀_K) )`.

Now `Ĥ⁰(G, Kˣ) = Fˣ / N_{K/F} Kˣ` (`Commons.RepresentationTheory.TateCohomology.
tateCohomology_zero_iso` with the Galois correspondence: the invariants of `Kˣ` are `Fˣ`) and
`Ĥ⁰(G, 𝕀_K) = 𝕀_F / N_{K/F} 𝕀_K` (the `G`-invariants of `𝕀_K` are the idèles of `F`:
`NK.GlobalCFT.Inclusion.globalCFT_fixed_idele_mem_range`,
`NK.GlobalCFT.Inclusion.globalCFT_idele_smul_eq_self_iff`,
`NK.GlobalCFT.Inclusion.globalCFT_ideleInclusion_exists`), the connecting map being induced by
the principal idèle `Fˣ ↪ 𝕀_F`.  Hence the kernel is `(Fˣ ∩ N_{K/F} 𝕀_K)/N_{K/F} Kˣ`.  The
infrastructure to build is the short exact sequence of `Rep ℤ G`'s and the identification of the
invariants of `𝕀_K`; `Commons.RepresentationTheory.TateCohomology.tateCohomology_iso_of_acyclic_middle`
and the homology sequence API around `TateCohomology.δ` do the homological part.

## Step 3 (`NK.CFTLib.normQuotient` elementwise description)

For a finite place `v` of `F` and `w | v`, the extension `K_w/F_v` is unramified, so by
`Commons.NumberTheory.NumberField.Completion.norm_units_surjective_of_ramificationIdx_eq_one` the
local units are norms, and `x ∈ F_vˣ` is a norm from `K_wˣ` iff `f_v ∣ v(x)`.  At an infinite
place the extension is `ℝ/ℝ` or `ℂ/ℂ`, so the local norm is surjective and there is no condition.
Hence

`x ∈ normPrincipalPreimage F K ↔ ∀ v, f_v ∣ v(x)`

which implies `(x) = N_{K/F}(𝔄)` for a fractional ideal `𝔄` of `K` (the divisor of `x` is the
norm of an integral divisor of `K`).

## Step 4 (`CFTLib/finrank_normQuotient_le`)

Let `m` be the exponent of the prime-to-`p` part of `Cl(K)` (so `m` is coprime to `p` and
`𝔠^m` has `p`-power order for every class `𝔠`).  For `x ∈ H := Fˣ ∩ N_{K/F} 𝕀_K` with
`(x) = N(𝔄)`, the class of `𝔄^m` has `p`-power order, so `hclass` writes

`𝔄^m = (y) · ∏_{w | T} 𝔓_w^{b_w}`.

Taking norms (`N(𝔓_w) = 𝔭_v` for `v ∈ T`, since `T` splits completely) gives

`x^m = ε · N(y) · π`,  with `ε ∈ (𝓞 F)ˣ` and `(π) = ∏_{v ∈ T} 𝔭_v^{a_v}`.

Both `ε · π` and `N(y)` lie in `H`, and `ε·π` lies in the `T`-units `E_{F,T} = Fˣ ∩ 𝕀_{F,T}`
(`NK.GlobalCFTdeep.Herbrand.TUnits`, `TIdeles`).  Therefore, for every `x ∈ H`, `x^m` has the same
image as an element of `H ∩ E_{F,T}` in `H/N_{K/F}Kˣ`.  Since `|G|` is a power of `p` and `m` is
coprime to `p`, the `m`-th power map is an automorphism of `H/N_{K/F}Kˣ`; consequently
`H/N_{K/F}Kˣ` is generated by the image of `H ∩ E_{F,T}`.  As `E_{F,T}/E_F` embeds into `ℤ^T`, the
group `E_{F,T}` — and hence its subgroup `H ∩ E_{F,T}` — is generated by `E_F` together with at
most `|T|` elements.  Finally `E_F ≅ μ_F × ℤ^{r₁+r₂-1}` (Dirichlet:
`NumberField.Units.exist_unique_eq_mul_prod`, `NumberField.Units.fundSystem`,
`NumberField.Units.rank = Fintype.card (InfinitePlace F) - 1`), and the torsion `μ_F` contributes to
the `𝔽_p`-dimension of the quotient only when `p ∣ #μ_F`, i.e. when `δ_p(F) = 1`.  Hence

`dim_{𝔽_p} (H/N_{K/F}Kˣ ⊗ 𝔽_p) ≤ (r₁ + r₂ - 1) + δ_p(F) + |T|`.

The annihilation of `H/N_{K/F}Kˣ` by `|G|` used in the second half of the argument follows from
steps 2, and from the general fact that `Ĥ⁻¹(G, M)` is killed by `|G|`
(`CFTLib/natCard_smul_tateCohomology_neg_one_eq_zero`); in the form needed here it is the lemma
`CFTLib/pow_natCard_mem_unitsNormSubgroup`.

## Assembly (in `Work/CFTLib/roquette_bound.lean`)

`tateCohomology_neg_one_iso_normQuotient` transports the computation to the concrete quotient, and
`NK.CFTLib.finrank_tensor_zmod_eq_of_linearEquiv` (in `roquetteDefs`) moves the `𝔽_p`-dimension
along the `ℤ`-linear isomorphism; `finrank_normQuotient_le` is the rank bound.
